//! Real analyzer implementations behind `AnalyticsEngine`.
//!
//! ## History
//!
//! Six of the nine analyzers `AnalyticsEngine::analyze` fans out to were
//! generated by a `create_analyzer_placeholder!` macro whose `analyze` body was
//! `pub async fn analyze(&self, _data: &[TimestampedMetrics]) -> Result<T> {
//! Ok($default_result) }` — the input was discarded and a hand-written literal
//! returned. A seventh, `AnomalyDetector`, was the same shape written out by
//! hand in `analytics/types.rs`. Between them they invented latency percentiles,
//! availability and MTTR/MTBF figures, SLA compliance rates, normality-test
//! statistics, forecast accuracy metrics and classifier precision/recall — all
//! of which `AnalyticsEngine` then folded into a single `confidence` score that
//! looked like a measurement.
//!
//! The macro is deleted. Every analyzer in this module computes its result from
//! the samples it is given, and returns a structured error naming the shortfall
//! when the window cannot support the analysis. Result fields that no metric
//! stream can support — availability, SLA compliance, energy and cost
//! efficiency, classifier accuracy, the Shapiro-Wilk statistic — are `Option`
//! and reported as `None`.

